c barrier so  there's a certain part of bullet ah that  is crossed sort of in the north part of  the center from north from the center of  town that is generally considered to be  that type of your regular city in all of  Latin America however once you get  outside of those areas the things get  very complicated you have illegal ways  of controlling every single economic  activity you have all kinds of informal  ways of providing security and again  illegal armed actors drug trafficking  other forms of organized crime also  affect the lives of these individuals so  we also have a lot of intra-city  displacement where people in meta-gene  have to go from one commune to the other  just because they do not get along with  the particular so there would seem to be  an absolute control also in the city by  the state authorities however that seems  to be more an ideal than in practice  unfortunately the situation in the  cities isn't that's great either our  standards of poverty as well there's  been a lot of debates of the changing  numbers but roughly we can say that  there's 30 percent of the people that  are affected by poverty I would say that  it's a lot greater unemployment and sub  employment is also much greater than  recognized officially and people are  just doing their daily their day in  order to survive just to make ends meet  on a daily basis and it's about survival  and it's about survival in a very  vicious very violent and very much led  by enemy and often again that's the  contradiction of Colombia we have laws  for everything we have you know a debate  on the new police code then we have a  debate on a law that resolves the  problems of those people that have been  forcibly displaced at the same time you  have a law that tries to regulate all of  urban action and all of urban life but  just stays in the books law and practice  really doesn't have that great of an  effect so why has the violence been  going on for so long and who is in  control today of 